  "why": "One server is one Node event loop and this CLI's turns are CPU rather than waiting, so carrying every session on one server misses the floor at four sessions where four separate agents pass. Retained in REJECTED.md. It does work: one app server serves the whole rung and peak RSS grows about 0.63 GiB a session rather than 1.0."
 },
 "revision": "zcode-speed-floor-confirmation-v1",
 "synthetic_model": true,
 "verdict": {
  "confirmed_sessions": 5,
  "driver_change_effect": "None that these measurements can resolve. Every published zcode rung uses one agent per session, where a server holds a single session and the serialization the candidate removes never had two turns to serialize.",
  "limit": "CPU inside the official CLI. One task pair costs about 8.1 CPU-seconds and peaks near 2.7 busy cores; the four-core quota is saturated from four sessions upward, and at eight sessions a rung's wall time is within about 11% of the floor that total CPU implies.",
  "why_not_six": "Six is on the bound and its verdict is not stable. The candidate's five-repeat confirmation gives 1.993 and the original's gives 2.027, but the candidate is not faster in absolute terms at six sessions (10.07s cold p50 against the original's 9.56s); the ratios differ because the two runs' own fresh serial controls differ by 11% (5.22s against 4.71s), which is larger than the gap between the verdicts. An exploratory three-repeat trial put six at 2.080. Five clears with margin in both variants and is what is published."
 }
}
